  [PASS] 10 AGAINST INTEREST -- X-ray ellipticals do NOT land on the RAR with a stellar-population M/L: they need MORE boost than the kernel gives, by a median factor of about 2 on both footings.  This is the same sign and roughly the same size as the group and cluster residual, in objects of only 1e11-1e12 Msun of stars.  The MOND literature reports the same thing for these systems (Milgrom 2012 needs the hot gas plus a raised M/L)   (canonical median (g_obs/g_bar)/nu(y) = 1.69 +- 0.249 dex; alt 1.57 +- 0.242 dex; y spans 0.02 to 11 across 5-70 kpc)
  [PASS] 10-shape ...and the shortfall is not a constant offset -- it GROWS toward low acceleration, which is what a missing extended mass component looks like and is not what a wrong M/L looks like (a wrong M/L would move the ratio most at HIGH y, where the stars dominate the field)   (d log[(g_obs/g_bar)/nu]/d log y = -0.231 over y = 0.02-11; ratio at the highest y = 0.91, at the lowest = 4.23)
  the M/L reading of the same numbers: the halo-plus-stars fits themselves prefer M*/L_K = 0.73 against the Kroupa prediction 1.10 (ratio 0.70) -- so the X-ray fits already want LESS stellar mass than stellar populations give, which makes the framework's shortfall worse, not better.
  the alternative computed beside: these same profiles were fitted with NFW + stars and needed M_dark/M_star = 98 within the virial radius, so this is not a case where LambdaCDM is comfortable and the framework is not -- it is a case where the framework removes most, but not all, of a very large discrepancy.
  [PASS] M10 mutation: with nu = 1 (no modification) the required boost is 1 everywhere and the SAME data give a discrepancy several times larger -- so the kernel is supplying most of the missing mass, just not all   (nu = 1 gives median g_obs/g_bar = 3.70 vs the kernel's residual 1.69)
  SCOPE, stated plainly: this uses the PUBLISHED BEST-FIT MODEL, not the raw deprojected temperature and density
  profiles, and it omits the hot gas mass (a few per cent inside 70 kpc, which would lower the residual slightly).
  It is therefore an indicative, not a definitive, elliptical-galaxy RAR test; the definitive version needs the
  deprojected rho(r), T(r) tables, which are published only as figures in that paper.

