  [PASS] 18 AGAINST INTEREST -- the item's hypothesis FAILS.  eta(r) is NOT one function of g_bar/a_0: over twelve clusters the residual is organised by r/R500 (0.10 dex) considerably BETTER than by the framework's own acceleration variable (0.17 dex), and the ordering survives a bootstrap over clusters.  A residual that is self-similar in the cluster's own radius, rather than a function of an acceleration, is what an extra MASS component looks like -- not what a deficiency of an acceleration-based kernel looks like   (canonical scatter of log eta = 0.167 dex at fixed g_bar/a_0 vs 0.102 dex at fixed r/R500 (alt 0.171 vs 0.103), equal-count quantile bins in both; g_bar/a_0 wins in only 0% of 500 cluster-bootstraps)
  [PASS] 18-value ...and the value it collapses to at R500 is close to the canonical 2 that the cluster front has carried for twenty years, on both footings and with the gas AND the stars measured rather than assumed   (canonical eta(0.9 R500) = 1.93 over 11 clusters (range 1.66-2.65); alt 1.70 (1.48-2.35))
  [PASS] 18-slope the curve is not flat: eta RISES toward higher g_bar/a_0, i.e. inward.  Extrapolated down to the galaxy regime the same curve would pass through eta = 1, but the clusters never reach acceleration low enough to check that here -- which is exactly why the group sample in item 7 mattered   (d log eta/d log(g_bar/a_0) = +0.185 +- (scatter 0.170 dex); g_bar/a_0 spans 0.064 to 0.463 over 0.2-1.1 R500)
  the alternative computed beside: the NEWTONIAN mass discrepancy on the same points has median 6.0 and is organised by g_bar/a_0 no worse -- but it is a factor 2.0 larger, and LambdaCDM has no reason for it to be a function of an acceleration at all.  Median M_star/M_gas in X-COP is 0.047, so the stellar mass is NOT the lever here.
  [PASS] M18-1 mutation: pairing each cluster's hydrostatic mass with ANOTHER cluster's baryons must destroy the collapse, and it does -- so the tightness above is a property of the clusters, not of the estimator   (shuffled scatter 0.259 +- 0.019 dex vs the real 0.167; 100% of 200 shuffles are worse)
  [PASS] M18-2 mutation: nu = 1 must give back the plain Newtonian discrepancy, and does   (nu = 1 gives eta = 6.0, the kernel gives 1.93)
  [PASS] M18-3 mutation AGAINST INTEREST -- a WRONG a_0 (ten times canonical) does NOT destroy the collapse.  So item 18 shows that the cluster residual is a smooth function of a baryonic acceleration; it does NOT measure a_0, and it must not be quoted as if it did.  The collapse is a statement about SHAPE, not about the constant   (scatter at 10 a_0 = 0.209 dex vs 0.167 dex at the canonical a_0 -- different)

